On February 10, 1971, Bright Tunes Music Corporation filed suit alleging that George Harrison had [[plagiarism|plagiarised]] "He's So Fine" in his then-current hit "My Sweet Lord". The case did not go to trial until February 1976, after which the judge ruled in favor of Bright Tunes on the liability portion of the suit, determining that Harrison had committed "subconscious" plagiarism.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://ccnmtl.columbia.edu/projects/law/library/cases/case_brightharrisongs.html |title=Bright Tunes Music v. Harrisongs Music |access-date=2007-09-05 |year=2002 |publisher=Columbia Law School Arthur W. Diamond Law Library Music Plagiarism Project |archive-date=2003-04-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030421014958/http://www.ccnmtl.columbia.edu/projects/law/library/cases/case_brightharrisongs.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The suit to determine damages was scheduled for November 1976 but delayed until February 1981, by which time [[Allen Klein]], Harrison's one-time manager, who had been his legal adviser in the first phase of the suit, had become the plaintiff by virtue of purchasing Bright Tunes. The final decision was that Harrison would purchase Bright Tunes from Klein for $587,000{{snd}} the amount Klein had paid for the corporation{{snd}} and although litigation continued for at least ten more years, that decision was upheld.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://abbeyrd.best.vwh.net/mysweet.htm |title=The "My Sweet Lord"/"He's So Fine" Plagiarism Suit |access-date=2008-11-23 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081221135306/http://abbeyrd.best.vwh.net/mysweet.htm |archive-date=2008-12-21 }}</ref> In 1975, the Chiffons recorded a version of "My Sweet Lord", attempting to capitalize on the publicity generated by the lawsuit. In 1976, Harrison released "[[This Song]]" in reaction to the plagiarism suit.

